<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The DITA twin of how-to.md. Same page, section for section: an H1 title, then
  Overview, Before you start, the task, and See also.

  Two things it demonstrates that the Markdown twin cannot. Structure comes
  from element nesting - the task is a <topic> nested inside the page's
  <topic>, not a heading with a bigger number - and the doctype is declared as
  a root-element attribute, which is where docmeta's XML extractor reads
  metadata from, so `type: how-to` routes this page the same way front matter
  routes the Markdown one.

  The <note> in Overview is in no bucket of the vocabulary mapping, so it and
  its <p> are skipped whole - the same rule that skips a Markdown blockquote.
  Overview therefore holds exactly one paragraph, as its twin does.
-->
<topic id="rotate-an-api-key" type="how-to">
  <title>Rotate an API key</title>
  <body>
    <section>
      <title>Overview</title>
      <p>Rotating a key replaces the old secret without interrupting live
        traffic.</p>
      <note type="tip">
        <p>An admonition is not a paragraph, so this one is skipped whole.</p>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Before you start</title>
      <p>You need an account with the <codeph>admin</codeph> role.</p>
    </section>
  </body>
  <topic id="rotate-the-key">
    <title>Rotate the key</title>
    <body>
      <p>Issue the replacement, then retire the old one.</p>
      <codeblock outputclass="language-bash">widget keys rotate --id abc123</codeblock>
    </body>
  </topic>
  <topic id="see-also">
    <title>See also</title>
    <body>
      <p>Read about key scopes and expiry.</p>
    </body>
  </topic>
</topic>
